.\"
.\" PTS(4) - Substrate Pseudo-Terminal Subsystem
.\"
.TH PTS 4 "2026-05-06" "Substrate OS" "Devices"
.SH NAME
ptmx, pts \- Unix98 pseudo-terminal multiplexer and slaves
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B /dev/ptmx
.B /dev/pts/<N>
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The Unix98 pseudo-terminal subsystem provides bidirectional pipes that
look like terminals to the slave end while the master end appears as
a raw byte stream.  A foreground process group on the slave receives
job-control signals exactly as it would on a real serial line.
.SS /dev/ptmx
Opening
.B /dev/ptmx
allocates a fresh master/slave pair.  Each open returns a distinct
master fd; multiple processes may open
.B /dev/ptmx
simultaneously.  The slave's index is queried with
.BR TIOCGPTN .
The master remains in a "locked" state — the slave cannot be opened —
until the caller issues
.BR TIOCSPTLCK
with a value of 0
.RB ( unlockpt (3)).
.SS /dev/pts/<N>
The slave behaves like a regular controlling terminal.  All termios
flags (canonical mode, echo, signal generation, software flow
control, etc.) apply normally.
.SH IOCTLS
.TP
.B TIOCGPTN
Get the slave's index.  Argument: pointer to
.BR int .
.TP
.B TIOCSPTLCK
Set the lock state.  Argument: pointer to
.BR int .
Non-zero locks the slave; zero unlocks it.
.TP
.B TIOCGPKT
Read the master's packet-mode flag.  Argument: pointer to
.BR int .
.TP
.B TIOCPKT
Set packet mode.  In packet mode each master read is prefixed with a
.B TIOCPKT_*
control byte.  Argument: pointer to
.BR int .
.TP
.B TIOCSIG
Send a signal to the slave's foreground process group.  Argument:
.BR int .
.TP
.B TCFLSH
Flush queued data from the master read buffer.  In packet mode,
sets the
.B TIOCPKT_FLUSHREAD
status bit so the master sees a flush event on its next read.
.SH PACKET-MODE CONTROL BYTES
.TP
.B TIOCPKT_DATA
The remainder of the read is normal data.
.TP
.B TIOCPKT_FLUSHREAD
The master read buffer was flushed.
.TP
.B TIOCPKT_FLUSHWRITE
Pending writes were discarded.
.TP
.B TIOCPKT_STOP
Output stopped (XOFF received).
.TP
.B TIOCPKT_START
Output resumed (XON received).
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /dev/ptmx
PTY multiplexer (open this to allocate a pair).
.TP
.B /dev/pts/
Directory of allocated slaves.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR posix_openpt (3),
.BR ioctl (2),
.BR tty (4),
.BR termios (3)
.SH HISTORY
The Unix98 PTY interface was introduced in SVR4 and adopted by POSIX.
